Guicciardini’s commitment to the particular, to the uniqueness of 
each situation, has two important consequences. One, of which he is 
fully aware, is a warning against overconfidence in commentators and, 
more crucially, in statesmen: arrogance is folly. The second, which 
informs the whole of Guicciardini’s history, is a commitment to expla- 
