beyond them (Fryde, Humanism and Renaissance Historiography). 
The growing vogue, in the later sixteenth century and all through the 
seventeenth, for Tacitus’ Annals and Histories as the most admired 
model, rather than Livy, introduced a welcome diversification, though 
the enthusiasm for Tacitus also expressed itself in collections of 
maxims of political conduct: he was safer to cite than Machiavelli. Some 
